    b"ml/ns_json/json.baml\x01#\x05\x00\x00Navigates a jq-style selector into a `json` value and c"
    b"oerces the leaf to\x0a`T`.\x0a\x0aSelectors start with `.` or `[`. Dot and quoted-bracket"
    b" steps address\x0aobject fields; integer bracket steps address arrays. A dot step r"
    b"uns to the\x0anext `.` or `[`, so a field name containing either must use the brack"
    b"et\x0aform: `[\"a.b\"]`. Bracket keys accept single or double quotes, and take `\\`\x0aas"
    b" a literal escape for one following character. Indices are non-negative\x0aand abso"
    b"lute \xe2\x80\x94 there is no jq-style negative indexing, and `[-1]` reports\x0aout of bound"
    b"s. A bare `.` selects nothing, so `\".\"` is the identity\x0aselector.\x0a\x0aThe leaf is c"
    b"oerced with `to<T>`, so `baml.FromJson` overrides apply and\x0a`T` may be a whole c"
    b"lass, not only a scalar. Every way this can fail \xe2\x80\x94\x0amalformed selector, missing"
    b" field, wrong shape, out-of-range index, leaf\x0athat will not decode \xe2\x80\x94 collapses"
    b" into one `PathError`, which is what makes\x0a`path_or` a clean total wrapper.\x0a\x0a# T"
    b"hrows\x0a\x0a- `baml.json.PathError` if the selector is malformed, if a step does not\x0a"
    b"  resolve, or if the selected leaf does not decode to `T`.\x0a\x0a# Examples\x0a\x0a```baml\x0a"
    b"let doc = baml.json.parse(\"{\\\"users\\\": [{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Ada\\\", \\\"age\\\": 36}]}\");\x0aas"
    b"sert.equal(baml.json.path<string>(doc, \".users[0].name\"), \"Ada\");\x0aassert.equal(b"
    b"aml.json.path<int>(doc, \"[\\\"users\\\"][0][\\\"age\\\"]\"), 36);\x0a```\x01\x04\x00\x00\x00path\x02\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x1a\x00\x00"
