    b"\x00\x00\x01\xa6\x04\x00\x00Lowers a BAML type to ordinary, provider-neutral JSON Schema.\x0a\x0aThe type-l"
    b"evel counterpart to the value conversions above: they move a\x0a*value* between BAM"
    b"L and `json`, while this describes the *shape* a value\x0aof `t` would take.\x0a\x0aOptio"
    b"nal fields are omitted from `required`, maps use schema-valued\x0a`additionalProper"
    b"ties`, and unions use `anyOf`. Enums lower to a string\x0a`enum` of their variant n"
    b"ames, `uint8array` to `string`, and `unknown` to\x0athe empty schema `{}`. A nullab"
    b"le type becomes a `[\"T\", \"null\"]` type list\x0awhere it can, and `anyOf` otherwise."
    b"\x0a\x0aThis pass *does* respect `@alias` and `@skip`, where the value conversions\x0ado "
    b"not \xe2\x80\x94 a schema is read by something outside BAML, so it must describe\x0athe wire"
    b" names. That is also why an enum variant lowers under its alias.\x0a\x0aThe root type "
    b"is emitted inline, and every class and named alias beneath it\x0agoes into `$defs` "
    b"to be reached by `$ref`. That is what lets a recursive\x0agraph terminate: a recurs"
    b"ive root is emitted both inline and into `$defs`,\x0aso the `$ref` back to itself r"
    b"esolves.\x0a\x0a# Throws\x0a\x0a- `baml.errors.Unsupported` for a type with no JSON Schema f"
    b"orm, and for a\x0a  class, enum, or alias the reflection context cannot resolve.\x01\x06\x00"
